WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.911 --> 00:00:05.695
Hello?

00:00:05.695 --> 00:00:06.876
Hello?

00:00:08.626 --> 00:00:10.207
I think you've got the wrong number.

00:00:10.207 --> 00:00:11.051
Hello?

00:00:11.051 --> 00:00:12.196
Okay.

00:00:12.358 --> 00:00:13.202
Bye!

00:00:30.838 --> 00:00:33.351
You just keep calling me don't you?

00:00:33.351 --> 00:00:36.727
Don’t you realize your TV is ruining
every single movie you watch?

00:00:36.727 --> 00:00:37.915
Uh.

00:00:37.915 --> 00:00:38.822
No.

00:00:39.837 --> 00:00:43.839
There’s a hidden setting that’s distorting
the color, the motion, the contrast

00:00:43.839 --> 00:00:45.285
that filmmakers want.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.371
Wait, what’s that noise?

00:00:48.738 --> 00:00:49.490
It's popcorn.

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:51.982
I only make popcorn when I’m about
to watch a movie.

00:00:53.449 --> 00:00:54.769
Everybody warned you about it.

00:00:54.769 --> 00:00:55.600
Tom did.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.347
"To that end, we'd like a moment of your time
to talk about video interpolation."

00:00:59.347 --> 00:01:01.129
The Always Sunny guys complained about it.

00:01:01.129 --> 00:01:03.100
"I don't understand how people can't see it."

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.412
"They can't see it!"

00:01:04.412 --> 00:01:08.067
“90% of the population does not know that
they’re watching auto motion plus.”

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:10.200
And your TV will still ruin your movie.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.149
What did you say?

00:01:12.149 --> 00:01:13.270
Check the settings.

00:01:14.211 --> 00:01:15.622
Check them.

00:01:18.482 --> 00:01:21.749
No, that’s not possible.

00:01:28.353 --> 00:01:30.157
This TV is a Samsung

00:01:30.157 --> 00:01:33.830
but every TV manufacturer has 
proprietary modes

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:35.722
they use to market their TVs.

00:01:35.722 --> 00:01:38.947
And they all distort the picture
in different ways.

00:01:38.947 --> 00:01:42.960
And the dreaded motion smoothing
is just the beginning.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.143
Have you had any personal experience 
with this problem?

00:01:47.143 --> 00:01:52.902
I distinctly remember this one experience
where I was at my parents house

00:01:52.902 --> 00:01:56.140
and we were just sitting around and watching
some movie

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:58.600
and I couldn't shake the feeling

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.969
that every single time that something
on the screen was moving

00:02:01.969 --> 00:02:05.166
there was this shadow trailing behind
people moving.

00:02:05.166 --> 00:02:10.069
Michael Zink is a VP of Emerging and Creative
Technologies at Warner Brothers

00:02:10.069 --> 00:02:13.087
but he’s also President of the UHD Alliance

00:02:13.087 --> 00:02:16.590
an industry group that brings together
lots of parties

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:20.838
to decide, basically, how TVs should
display stuff.

00:02:20.838 --> 00:02:22.709
So if you walk into somebody's house

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:26.250
are you like bound to say something
like a superhero, almost?

00:02:26.758 --> 00:02:30.551
Yeah, you put on your cape and then you grab
the remote.

00:02:30.551 --> 00:02:34.033
The UHD Alliance formed in 2015.

00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:36.966
Filmmakers noticed big new problems

00:02:36.966 --> 00:02:42.962
as HDTVs were being turned into 4K beasts
with lots of extra features.

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:45.409
They like to compete and show off all
the bells and whistles

00:02:45.409 --> 00:02:48.125
and how they’re better than their competitors.

00:02:48.125 --> 00:02:51.787
And that’s all well and good, but...

00:02:52.953 --> 00:02:55.000
Not necessarily when you’re watching a movie.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.440
You can see them in these short clips

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.439
from the trailers for last three
Best Cinematography winners.

00:03:01.439 --> 00:03:03.170
I filmed them on my TV

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:07.697
with typical TV adjustments and
with none at all.

00:03:07.697 --> 00:03:13.479
In "1917", the color saturations are totally
different and brightness is cranked up.

00:03:13.479 --> 00:03:17.828
See how in "Mank", the brightness levels
are totally different?

00:03:17.828 --> 00:03:21.869
Here’s the darkest and brightest points
in the scene, side by side.

00:03:21.869 --> 00:03:26.430
And in Dune, you can really see
the digitally sharpened details

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.733
and cranked saturation.

00:03:28.733 --> 00:03:33.460
These choices overwrite what the
best cinematographers and colorists wanted.

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:36.430
And they are baked into TVs.

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:41.527
Typically movies are shot or mastered
in a mastering suite

00:03:41.527 --> 00:03:45.119
and once the movies are finished...

00:03:45.119 --> 00:03:47.819
that's how the director wants them
to be seen.

00:03:50.754 --> 00:03:52.412
Hey! How's it going?

00:03:54.256 --> 00:03:55.974
Hey, what's up Phil?

00:03:55.974 --> 00:03:59.260
I was hoping you could show me one of the
edit bays for this video.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:01.813
Yea, sure, one second.

00:04:01.813 --> 00:04:04.887
Movies are finished in dark rooms
kind of like these

00:04:04.887 --> 00:04:07.263
probably with even fancier gear.

00:04:11.616 --> 00:04:14.755
Hey, I'm realizing your voice sounds
a little familiar

00:04:14.755 --> 00:04:16.079
from a call I got last night...

00:04:18.431 --> 00:04:20.665
What do you mean?

00:04:24.052 --> 00:04:27.280
You can manually adjust white balance
and other settings

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:31.211
but TV menus make it maddeningly complex.

00:04:31.211 --> 00:04:34.380
Even the name for these baked in settings
is confusing:

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:38.170
“Standard picture mode” implies
basic, straightforward.

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:43.288
But usually, that mode is packed full
of these tweaked settings.

00:04:43.288 --> 00:04:49.181
But the worst offender, by far, is the motion
and that’s also the hardest to show.

00:04:49.181 --> 00:04:51.609
Here's the thing about explaining
motion smoothing.

00:04:51.609 --> 00:04:54.320
If I were to publish this video at
60 frames a second to show you

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:58.060
what that looks like, you might be watching
it on YouTube at 24 frames a second

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:01.162
so the entire point of it would be
completely lost.

00:05:01.162 --> 00:05:03.890
And that doesn't account for the fact that
when I do publish this video

00:05:03.890 --> 00:05:08.190
at 24 frames a second, you might be watching
it on a "Dynamic Mode" on your TV

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:11.060
at 60 frames a second — artificially.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:14.040
Which means it'll look totally different
and that doesn't even account for the fact

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:17.830
that there's going to be exporting issues
when I put this out of a software program

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:21.100
and compression involved with the codec 
which is playing it back to you

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:22.703
on the internet...

00:05:22.703 --> 00:05:24.105
It's hard.

00:05:27.172 --> 00:05:28.893
There are some workarounds.

00:05:28.893 --> 00:05:33.517
This ball is moving at 24 frames a second,
like this entire video.

00:05:33.517 --> 00:05:38.297
This one is moving at 12 frames a second,
half the frames.

00:05:38.297 --> 00:05:40.815
See the big difference in the motion?

00:05:40.815 --> 00:05:44.590
That's as big as what your TV does
when it doubles the frames

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:47.323
in a 24 frames per second movie.

00:05:47.323 --> 00:05:50.250
Here's every frame for that 24 frame
per second ball.

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:55.000
And here's every frame if it moved at
a typical 60 frames a second.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:58.007
Do you see the huge difference
in information?

00:05:58.007 --> 00:06:01.846
And your TV is often just making up
these frames.

00:06:01.846 --> 00:06:05.474
You can also see what's going on
by filming TVs.

00:06:05.474 --> 00:06:09.844
These frames are taken from a TV in
"Standard Mode"...

00:06:09.844 --> 00:06:13.804
while these ones are totally unchanged.

00:06:13.804 --> 00:06:16.171
This too meta for you, by the way?

00:06:16.171 --> 00:06:20.415
We are used to seeing movies at around 24
frames a second.

00:06:20.415 --> 00:06:22.283
Anything not that frame rate

00:06:22.283 --> 00:06:24.910
60 frames a second, or even 30

00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:28.803
won't have the same gaps between frames
that we're used to.

00:06:28.803 --> 00:06:35.649
They are 24 frames per second files
but I filmed my TV at 60 frames per second.

00:06:35.649 --> 00:06:37.780
See how in the unchanged side

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:41.729
there’s movement from this frame
to this frame.

00:06:41.729 --> 00:06:46.380
Now look over here: the TV has just added
a frame in the middle

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:49.574
in addition to all those tweaks
to the picture.

00:06:49.574 --> 00:06:53.870
This isn’t what we’re used to movies looking
like, for the very simple fact that

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:58.595
we’re used to movies only showing frames
they wanted to show us.

00:06:58.595 --> 00:07:01.627
This frame in an example of motion smoothing.

00:07:01.627 --> 00:07:05.910
It invents frames, which makes motion look
different from a movie.

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:09.180
It also creates weird, barely 
perceptible artifacts

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:11.610
because the software just isn’t good enough.

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:15.246
24 frame per second videos have this
which is motion blur.

00:07:15.246 --> 00:07:21.370
Motion smoothing invents weird
digital artifacts instead.

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:23.560
But we aren't stuck with it.

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:28.120
The UHD Alliance works to set a lot of standards
for TV

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:31.750
but among those standards are the relatively
new Filmmaker Mode

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:35.190
a TV setting with the same name, across brands

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:38.140
that basically turns off all the crap.

00:07:38.686 --> 00:07:43.249
The notion behind it is, we will maintain
whatever the original frame rate is.

00:07:43.249 --> 00:07:47.809
So if your content is in 48 or 60 or 120
or whatever that is...

00:07:47.809 --> 00:07:50.472
that's the frame rate that TV should reproduce.

00:07:50.472 --> 00:07:55.580
What we don't want is to add additional frames 
or eliminate certain frames.

00:07:55.580 --> 00:08:00.291
It's really making sure that whatever is in
the signal stays the way it is

00:08:00.291 --> 00:08:02.860
and is represented on the TV
in a faithful manner.

00:08:03.977 --> 00:08:05.632
I'm not doing this anymore.

00:08:05.632 --> 00:08:07.392
Don't change that setting!

00:08:07.392 --> 00:08:08.470
I have a choice!

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:09.632
Don't click that menu, Philip!

00:08:09.632 --> 00:08:10.900
Don't you dare!

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:17.800
One of one of our colleagues at the UHDA

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:20.960
he always compared what 
we're trying to do.

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:22.540
It's always this analogy of

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:28.389
if you go to a high end steakhouse
and you order a really expensive steak.

00:08:28.389 --> 00:08:32.231
You want it to be brought to you the way
the chef prepares it.

00:08:32.231 --> 00:08:37.120
You don't want the steak to arrive with a
server deciding halfway through that, hey,

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:41.123
let me just put a whole bunch of ketchup on
top of that because that's how I like to eat.

00:08:41.123 --> 00:08:44.430
And that's very similar to what 
these TVs are doing.

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:47.778
They decide for you that, hey,
this is great content,

00:08:47.778 --> 00:08:49.089
but let me shopping it a little bit.

00:08:49.089 --> 00:08:50.458
Let me reduce some noise.

00:08:50.458 --> 00:08:51.740
Let me change the colors.

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:52.740
Oh, and by the way,

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:55.403
let me invent a couple of frames
in between as well.

00:08:55.403 --> 00:09:00.411
And if a director would want to have done
that, they would have done that.

00:09:00.411 --> 00:09:04.040
But it shouldn't be up to the television to
decide to do this.

